---
<DocsPage id="engineering-records" title="Engineering records" description="The engineering case studies of Agent Codeyard: features of Codeyard itself, built with its own workflow, each named by the problem it solved, with its figures, review rounds and pull request." toc={toc}>
  <p>Features of Codeyard itself, built with its own workflow, each named by the problem it solved. A short summary first; open the records for the figures, the review rounds and the excerpts. Issue numbers name the sources. The products built with Codeyard are on <a href={href("examples/")}>Projects</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="memory">When the reviewer did not know what was decided</h2>
  <p class="needs">Source: <a href={issue(217)}>Issue #217</a>, <a href={pull(222)}>PR #222</a>. One unattended run of <code>run-feature.sh</code>, 28 October 2026.</p>
  <p>Each review round started without the decisions of the rounds before it. So the reviewer raised findings again that were already fixed or accepted, and every repeat cost a round. The change gives every later round the earlier decisions. Its own review took five rounds: round 3 found that the first fix could still let a raised finding lose its severity. That was fixed, and the owner merged it with the required approval.</p>
  <details class="proof">
    <summary>Records: figures, agents, review rounds, the run and the pull request</summary>
    <CaseSummary>
      <Fragment slot="changed">Every review round after the first receives the decisions of the earlier rounds, and a finding raised again names the one it continues.</Fragment>
      <Fragment slot="rounds">5. Rounds 1 and 3 each raised a major finding, fixed; round 5 passed with one minor finding, accepted.</Fragment>
      <Fragment slot="found">In round 3, that the fix of round 1 could still let a raised finding lose its severity, which would have weakened the very check the Issue protects.</Fragment>
      <Fragment slot="result"><a href={pull(222)}>PR #222</a>, merged by the owner with the required approval, 29 October, 01:14 UTC.</Fragment>
    </CaseSummary>
    <dl class="facts">
      <dt>tokens</dt><dd>about 1.7 million in all, about 1.1 million of them for review and fixes. Tokens are the units of text the models read, as the providers report them, cached reads included. Source: the run record.</dd>
      <dt>interventions</dt><dd>none recorded. An intervention is something the owner did during the run that a script recorded, such as resuming a session by hand. Source: the run record.</dd>
      <dt>active run time</dt><dd>1 h 30 min, from the start of the run, 21:38 UTC, to the pull request, 23:08. Source: the run record.</dd>
      <dt>waiting time</dt><dd>About 2 h 6 min, nearly all of it after the pull request opened, until the merge.</dd>
      <dt>total lead time</dt><dd>3 h 36 min, from the opening of the Issue, 21:37 UTC, to the merge, 29 October, 01:14. Source: the timestamps on GitHub.</dd>
    </dl>
    <p class="lede">Each review round started without the decisions of the rounds before it. So the reviewer raised findings again that had already been fixed, or that the triage had accepted, and every such repeat cost a round. #217 gave every later round the earlier decisions.</p>

    <h3>The problem, from the run records</h3>
    <p>The run analysis of 28 October 2026, in which a read-only agent reads the records of earlier runs, found that in five runs the reviewer raised a finding again as unresolved after it had been fixed, and that in one feature it raised findings again that the triage had accepted. The Issue asks that every review after round 1 receives the decisions of all earlier rounds, that a finding names the one it continues (<code>unresolved</code> or <code>worsened</code>), and that the stored review is checked against the recorded decisions. It has six acceptance criteria; the last one reads “No gate is weakened: a FIX_NOW finding that is really unresolved is still reported and still blocks.” Out of scope: the triage rules, the round limit, and the owner-exception mechanism.</p>

    <h3>Risk level and agents</h3>
    <dl class="facts">
      <dt>risk</dt><dd><span class="lvl high">high</span>: the feature changes <code>.agents/prompts/reviewer.md</code> and <code>.agents/schemas/feature-review.schema.json</code>, existing contract files; <code>scripts/lib/review-data.sh</code> and <code>scripts/review-feature.sh</code>, listed as high in <code>risk.conf</code>; and four existing test files. The reviewer also classified the impact on the architecture as major.</dd>
      <dt>implementer</dt><dd><code>claude opus</code>. The first session started before there was a diff, so the level was still the default, medium. That gap became <a href={issue(227)}>#227</a>: you can now raise the level before the first session (<a href={pull(229)}>PR #229</a>).</dd>
      <dt>fixes</dt><dd><code>claude fable</code>, selected by the line <code>triage-implementer@high</code></dd>
      <dt>review</dt><dd><code>codex gpt-6-luna</code>, read-only</dd>
      <dt>triage</dt><dd><code>claude sonnet</code>, read-only</dd>
    </dl>

    <h3>Implementation and tests</h3>
    <p>The change adds the earlier decisions to the reviewer's context in <code>scripts/review-feature.sh</code>, the optional <code>previous</code> field to the finding schema, and the validation in <code>review_artifact_errors</code> of <a href={repoFile("scripts/lib/review-data.sh")}><code>scripts/lib/review-data.sh</code></a>. A new test file, <a href={repoFile("tests/review-data-test.sh")}><code>tests/review-data-test.sh</code></a>, covers each invalid case; existing tests only gained blocks, no assertion was changed. Each re-raise becomes a <code>finding</code> event in the run record, and <code>report-runs.sh</code> counts them per feature.</p>

    <h3>Independent review and triage</h3>
    <Table>
      <caption>Rounds 1, 3 and 5 reviewed the complete feature, 2 and 4 only the fixes. The triage of each round with findings is published on the Issue, marked as approved by an unattended run.</caption>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col" class="num">round</th><th scope="col">reviewed</th><th scope="col">verdict</th><th scope="col">finding</th><th scope="col">decision</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {rounds.map((row) => (
          <tr>
            <th scope="row" class="num">{row.link ? <a href={row.link}>{row.round}</a> : row.round}</th>
            <td data-col="reviewed">{row.kind}</td>
            <td data-col="verdict" class="k">{row.verdict}</td>
            <td data-col="finding" class:list={{ off: row.finding === "–" }}>{row.finding}</td>
            <td data-col="decision" class:list={["k", { off: row.decision === "–" }]}>{row.decision}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </Table>
    <p>Round 3 is the review doing its job: the fix of round 1 was correct, but the new rule could still let a re-raised finding lower its severity, which would have weakened the very gate the Issue protects. Round 5 found one minor point, and the triage accepted it with a rationale, quoted below.</p>
    <pre role="region" class="excerpt" aria-label="Excerpt of the triage of round 5"><span class="src">{issue(217).replace(/^https:\/\//, "")} · round 5 · triage, excerpt</span>### MIN1. The review schema makes `previous` required

- Severity: minor
- Rationale: Listing `previous` in `required` while allowing null
is deliberate. It follows the strict structured-output
convention, where every property is required and optionality
is expressed as nullable. [...] The runtime validators accept
artifacts that omit it, so old artifacts stay valid [...]
The mismatch is cosmetic and blocks nothing.</pre>

    <h3>Verification evidence</h3>
    <p>The full verification ran three times, once after each writing session: 1046, 1049 and 1118 seconds. They included the workflow's self-tests, because #217 changed workflow files; the header of <a href={repoFile("scripts/verify-workflow.conf")}><code>verify-workflow.conf</code></a> records that suite at 915 seconds when its budget was set. Eight more times a step required a verification and reused the recorded pass, because the content had not changed since. Every review report names the reviewed tree and the verification that passed for it.</p>

    <h3>The run</h3>
