      <dt>total lead time</dt><dd>13 h 46 min, from the opening of the Issue, 06:23 UTC, to the merge, 20:09. Source: the timestamps on GitHub.</dd>
    </dl>
    <p class="needs">This is one recorded run on the maintainer's server, not a benchmark. Another change, model or machine takes a different time.</p>

    <h3>The run in detail</h3>
    <p>The same run with the records each step left, for readers who want the mechanics.</p>
    <dl class="facts">
      <dt>risk</dt><dd><span class="lvl medium">medium</span>. The risk level is what the scripts compute from the files a change touches; it decides which model writes the fixes and whether the merge needs the owner's approval. The four changed files, three pages and the style sheet, are named by no class of <code>risk.conf</code>, so each gets the default. No escalation applied, the reviewer classified the impact on the architecture as minor, and the triage was full, the default.</dd>
      <dt>implementer</dt><dd><code>claude opus</code>, the plain line <code>implementer</code> of <code>agents.conf</code></dd>
      <dt>fixes</dt><dd><code>claude opus</code>, the plain line <code>triage-implementer</code>; at high, as in #217, <code>claude fable</code></dd>
      <dt>review</dt><dd><code>codex gpt-6-luna</code>, read-only</dd>
      <dt>triage</dt><dd><code>claude sonnet</code>, read-only</dd>
    </dl>
    <Table>
      <caption>Rounds 1 and 3 reviewed the complete feature, round 2 only the fix. The triage of round 1 is published on the Issue, marked as approved by an unattended run.</caption>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col" class="num">round</th><th scope="col">reviewed</th><th scope="col">verdict</th><th scope="col">finding</th><th scope="col">decision</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {storyRounds.map((row) => (
          <tr>
            <th scope="row" class="num">{row.link ? <a href={row.link}>{row.round}</a> : row.round}</th>
            <td data-col="reviewed">{row.kind}</td>
            <td data-col="verdict" class="k">{row.verdict}</td>
            <td data-col="finding" class:list={{ off: row.finding === "–" }}>{row.finding}</td>
            <td data-col="decision" class:list={["k", { off: row.decision === "–" }]}>{row.decision}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </Table>
    <p>The verification, the project's full set of checks in <code>verify.sh</code>, ran twice, after the implementation and after the fix: 14 and 15 seconds, of which 13 were the check of this site. The workflow's own self-tests in <a href={repoFile("scripts/verify-workflow.conf")}><code>verify-workflow.conf</code></a> run only when a workflow file changes, and #241 changed none. Five more times a step reused the recorded pass, because the content had not changed since.</p>
    <pre role="region" class="excerpt" aria-label="Excerpt of the description of pull request 249"><span class="src">{pull(249).replace(/^https:\/\//, "")} · excerpt</span>Review: round 3, PASS, by codex (gpt-6-luna); triage published on #241

Merge approval:
- not required
- the independent review classified the impact on the architecture as minor, and no protected path is changed
[...]
Risk: medium
[...]
- profile: none</pre>
  </details>

  <h2 id="tradeoff">How many review rounds are worth it</h2>
  <p class="needs">From the run records of <a href={issue(204)}>#204</a>, <a href={issue(241)}>#241</a> and <a href={issue(217)}>#217</a>. <a href="#sources">Sources</a>.</p>
  <p>Every review round is another call of the reviewer, and every finding that must be fixed sends the builder back to work. The rounds repeat until one passes, and stop for the owner after five. In the three runs compared here, one round that found nothing took seconds; five rounds that found two real problems took about half of the run.</p>
  <details class="proof">
    <summary>Records: rounds, time and tokens of three runs</summary>
    <Table>
      <caption>Review time: from the start of round 1 to the end of the last review or triage, fixes included. Tokens: the input tokens of every reviewer, triage and fix call, as the providers report them, cached reads included.</caption>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">run</th><th scope="col" class="num">rounds</th><th scope="col">what the rounds found</th><th scope="col">review and fixes</th><th scope="col">active run time</th><th scope="col">tokens of review and fixes</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {costs.map((row) => (
          <tr>
            <th scope="row"><a href={`#${row.anchor}`}>#{row.issue}</a></th>
            <td data-col="rounds" class="num">{row.rounds}</td>
