  <h2 id="repair">When a missed target sent the agent to repair</h2>
  <p class="needs">Source: <a href={issue(193)}>Issue #193</a>, <a href={pull(199)}>PR #199</a>, 27 October 2026.</p>
  <p>A check against a time or size target failed the verification when the target was missed, and the builder was sent to repair it without a diagnosed problem. The change reports such a check as a measurement instead. The review raised the same major finding twice: the first fix still treated an exit status that a measurement can return as a failure. The second fix held.</p>
  <details class="proof">
    <summary>Records: figures, review rounds and the changed files</summary>
    <CaseSummary>
      <Fragment slot="changed">A check against a target for a duration or a size became a measurement that is reported, not repaired.</Fragment>
      <Fragment slot="rounds">4. Rounds 1 and 2 raised findings, all fixed; rounds 3 and 4 passed.</Fragment>
      <Fragment slot="found">In round 2, the major finding of round 1 again, as unresolved: the fix treated an exit status that a measurement can return as a failure.</Fragment>
      <Fragment slot="result"><a href={pull(199)}>PR #199</a>, merged by the owner with the required approval, 27 October, 19:48 UTC.</Fragment>
    </CaseSummary>
    <dl class="facts">
      <dt>tokens</dt><dd>about 7.2 million in all, about 1.0 million of them for review and fixes. Source: the run record.</dd>
      <dt>interventions</dt><dd>none recorded. Source: the run record.</dd>
      <dt>active run time</dt><dd>1 h 18 min, from the start of the run to the pull request. Source: the run record.</dd>
      <dt>waiting time</dt><dd>About 5 h 59 min, before the run started and until the merge together.</dd>
      <dt>total lead time</dt><dd>7 h 17 min, from the opening of the Issue, 12:31 UTC, to the merge. Source: the timestamps on GitHub.</dd>
    </dl>
    <p class="lede">A check that measures a duration or a size against a target failed the verification when the target was missed, and the scripts sent the implementer to repair it, with no diagnosed problem. #193 made such budget checks measurements that are reported, not repaired.</p>
    <p>In round 1 the reviewer found that a failing timeout utility was reported as an exceeded budget (major) and that a budget's output was cut short (minor); the triage decided to fix both. In round 2 the reviewer raised the major finding again, as “Unresolved M1”: the fix now treated exit status 128 as a failure of the call, though a measurement can return it. A second fix round resolved it, and rounds 3 and 4 passed. Each round's report and triage is <a href={issue(193)}>on the Issue</a>.</p>
    <p>Source files changed by <a href={pull(199)}>PR #199</a>: {changed[199].map((path, i) => (<>{i > 0 && ", "}<a href={repoFile(path)}><code>{path}</code></a></>))}.</p>
  </details>

  <h2 id="short">When one round is enough</h2>
  <p class="needs">Source: <a href={pull(205)}>PR #205</a> and <a href={pull(213)}>PR #213</a>, 28 October 2026.</p>
  <p class="lede">Most features do not need several rounds. These two had a clear problem and a clear request, and the first review passed.</p>
  <h3>When the reviewer saw untouched records as deleted</h3>
  <p>The reviewer was shown the workflow's own approval records as deleted, though the change never touched them. The change hides from the reviewer the paths that the content fingerprint leaves out. One implementation session, one review, nothing found. Source: <a href={issue(204)}>Issue #204</a>.</p>
