  <h2 id="levels">What each risk level changed</h2>
  <p class="needs">The levels as each run recorded them. <a href={href("docs/risk-policy/")}>Risk policy</a>.</p>
  <p>The risk level of a feature decides which model writes its fixes and whether the merge needs the owner's approval. This repository's risk policy has no low class, no low default and no profile line, so every feature here is medium or high.</p>
  <details class="proof">
    <summary>Records: the examples by level</summary>
    <Table>
      <caption>From the risk lines of each pull request description and the run records. Active run time: from the start of <code>run-feature.sh</code> to the pull request.</caption>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">level</th><th scope="col">example</th><th scope="col">why this level</th><th scope="col" class="num">rounds</th><th scope="col">active run time</th><th scope="col">merge approval</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row"><span class="lvl high">high</span></th><td data-col="example"><a href="#memory">#217</a>, <a href={pull(222)}>PR #222</a></td><td data-col="why this level">It changes the reviewer's contract, the review schema and gate scripts</td><td data-col="rounds" class="num">5</td><td data-col="active run time">1 h 30 min</td><td data-col="merge approval">required</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row"><span class="lvl medium">medium</span></th><td data-col="example"><a href="#story">#241</a>, <a href={pull(249)}>PR #249</a></td><td data-col="why this level">Website pages that no class of the policy names: the default</td><td data-col="rounds" class="num">3</td><td data-col="active run time">13 min</td><td data-col="merge approval">not required</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row"><span class="lvl">low</span></th><td data-col="example" class="off">–</td><td data-col="why this level">Only where the risk policy makes the changed paths low: a <code>low:</code> class, <code>default: low</code> or <code>profile: light</code>. This repository has none</td><td data-col="rounds" class="num off">–</td><td data-col="active run time" class="off">–</td><td data-col="merge approval" class="off">–</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </Table>
    <p>Under the shipped policy, medium and high get the same rounds: the full verification, a review of the changes only when a round can build on the one before, and the full triage. What the level changed between these two runs is the model of the fixes and the owner's approval of the merge. Physics by Construction is not in this table: it names its levels 1 to 3 in its own policy, and <a href={href("examples/#application")}>its case</a> shows them as it recorded them.</p>
